The short version of what changed: the classic loaders are dead because they are **string-signatured**, not because the underlying primitive stopped working. `amsiInitFailed` reflection, the raw `AmsiScanBuffer` prologue patches posted to every blog since 2016, and the `E_INVALIDARG` one-shot are all matched by Defender content signatures on the *script text* — AMSI scans the bypass before it can neuter AMSI. The fix is to (a) never let the signatured token appear literally in the input, and (b) detour the native function by **documented ABI** rather than by build-specific byte offset, so the loader doesn't need re-authoring every patch Tuesday.

## What is dead and why

| Loader | Failure mode in 2025 |
|---|---|
| `[Ref].Assembly.GetType('System.Management.Automation.AmsiUtils').GetField('amsiInitFailed','NonPublic,Static').SetValue($null,$true)` | The type name + field name are one signature; the scan happens on the same line that would set the field, so the block fires before execution. PS 7.3+ additionally removed the short-circuit. |
| `xor eax,eax; ret` alone on `AmsiScanBuffer` | Returns `S_OK` but never writes the 6th argument (`AMSI_RESULT*`). The caller's out-slot holds stack garbage; `AmsiResultIsMalware = 1`, so you get intermittent blocks. |
| `mov eax,0x80070057; ret` | Relies on the host failing open on `HRESULT != S_OK`. True for stock PS 5.1 `AmsiUtils.ScanContent`, not true for every host/EDR AMSI provider. |
| `VirtualProtect` to `RWX` and leaving it | `.text` stays writable; behaviour-monitoring flags the page state, not just the write. |
| Any loader containing the literal strings | Blocked on arrival. |

The primitive that survives is a **result-rewriting detour**: overwrite `AmsiScanBuffer` so it writes `AMSI_RESULT_CLEAN` through its own out-pointer and returns `S_OK`. That is indistinguishable from a legitimate clean verdict — there is no error for the caller or an EDR AMSI provider to interpret. It depends only on the x64 ABI, not on any build's prologue bytes.

